// NEGATIVE TEST -- a value-returning function with no value on some path is a
// compile error (N3-03).
//
// THE SHAPE THIS FILE PINS is the one no reader sees as a defect: `f` returns a
// value on the true path and simply ends on the false one. There is no missing
// keyword, no unreachable code and no type error -- the body is well-formed and
// the last statement is an `if`. What is wrong is a PATH.
//
// WHAT THE OLD BEHAVIOUR WAS, and why an exit code cannot see it: code
// generation emitted a non-void C function that falls off its end, so `f(0)`
// returned whatever was in the return register. The program compiled, linked,
// ran, exited 0 and printed a number. That is the class M1 closed, and the
// reason this is a `reject` fixture rather than a `run` one with an expected
// transcript: there is no correct output to record for a program whose answer
// is register contents.
//
// COVERAGE SPLIT, stated so this file is not read as the whole rule: the other
// three shapes -- an empty body, a body whose last statement ends in a
// semicolon, and a `loop` that may not be entered -- are pinned by
// tests/m1_missing_return.rs, which asserts the rendered diagnostic. This
// fixture is the corpus-level half: the refusal survives in the compiler the
// conformance gate actually runs.
fn f(n: i64) -> i64 {
    if n > 0 {
        return 1;
    }
}

fn main() {
    print_int(f(1));
    print_int(f(0));
}
